# KV5-4, the Mac, 2026-11-05: the slot views go. Each layer's K, V and int8 scale buffers are its pools, and a slot is only its block table. Shared lock throughout; no timed read (no kernel changed).

== what changed
model.go: the pools are allocated without a slot offset; kvSlotBuf is a slot's table alone; useKVSlot binds the table (r.kt) and nothing else. kvScaleOff and kvHostOff leave production: host code goes through kvHostRow, and the two survive in testshared_test.go for tests that read an in-order slot.
batch.go: the step's per-row binding (Gemma 4's whole-layer rows, the gated-attention rows) swaps only the table; the step's int8 rows and pools bind the layer's pool.
kvcopy.go: CopyKV's pools are the layer's buffers.
The pools' bytes are unchanged, so ResidentKVBytes, the fit and the memory gate do not move.

== G1a whole: TestKVPages_everyResidentFixture (backend/metal, -tags goinfer_testhooks)
Every tiny fixture under testdata and decoder/testdata with a config and weights, copied with its window raised to 2,048 positions, loaded at -ctx 1024 with 3 KV slots and every batched step on (MoE, recurrent, Gemma 4), in-order against scrambled tables.
Per slot: a 300-token prompt (PrefillLast), 64 decodes, a second turn of 70 rows from position 364 (inside a block), a 4-row verify (ForwardN); then the batched step over up to three slots.
Held: every logit bit-identical; the same cases refused by both arms; the same prefill attention launches (prefillAttnRanForTest). Each step row is also held to a decode of the same token on its own slot, from the same history in another build, since a row bound to another slot's table is a mistake both arms share.
Result, 33 of 54 fixtures compared, all PASS. 21 NOT RUN, each because Metal declines the fixture or the decoder does not load it (vision and audio towers, an embedding model): listed in the test's output. The step ran on 21 fixtures. Prefill kernels covered: attention_prefill, attention_prefill_fused, attention_prefill_steel, attention_prefill_steel_hd64. Six fixtures (MLA, Gemma 4 MoE and two-geometry, olmo_hybrid, kimi) refuse their 6 verify rows identically in both arms.
First reads, kept here because each changed the test:
  - At each fixture's own window, 26 fixtures refused every case (prompts past their caches) and the comparison passed vacuously; the long copies fixed it, and a fixture that builds a resident but runs no case now fails.
  - With no step options set, the Gemma 4 and recurrent steps never built; the test now turns every step on.
  - My first decode reference appended Forward's slice, the resident's logits buffer, so every reference row read the last one's logits; it clones now.

== plants, 4 of 4 red (18:10 to 18:16)
useKVSlot keeping the previous table: 61 failing tests. The Gemma 4 step's rows bound to slot 0's table: TestKVPages_everyResidentFixture and TestMC3Step_gemma4BitIdentical. The gated-attention rows bound to slot 0's table: TestMC3Step_recurrentBitIdentical. The step's fused rows reading slot 0's table: TestKVPages_fusedAttentionDepthThroughTheTable.
The first two runs had the step plants green: before the steps were on, and before the decode reference, both arms shared the wrong slot.

== Gemini's point on #694
TestKVPages_prefillKernelsReadTheTable now perturbs the value row at each checked position as well as the key row, so an offset inside a block on the V side cannot be shared by both arms. Green on all 8 kernels.

== RETRACTED: the recurrent step against decode
This record's first version reported, as open and on main too, that on the six recurrent hybrids a batched step row differs from its slot's decode. That was a bug in G1a's reference arm: it ran the batched step and then decoded each row, discarding the step's rows. On an attention-only model the decode rewrites the same KV row, so nothing showed; on a recurrent model the step had already advanced each slot's recurrent state, and the reference decoded one token ahead. The reference arm no longer runs the step.
With that fixed, G1a holds every step row to its slot's decode on all 21 step fixtures, the six recurrent hybrids included: PASS.
Measured before the cause was found, at the coordinator's request, by TestMC3Step_recurrentMatchesDecodeOnServeHistories (backend/metal), which builds each slot's history the way serve does: on qwen35-tiny, qwen3next-tiny, qwen3_5-tiny and nemotron-tiny, with histories by single-token decode, by PrefillLast, by ForwardBatch, and with the step's own slot bound, then 40 greedy tokens on twin slots: max |step - decode| 0, argmax 80 of 80, greedy tokens agree 40 of 40, in all 16 cells. The step is bit-identical to decode; --recurrent-step's default is not implicated. No real recurrent model was run for it.

